{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-38615" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $T:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}-2x+y \\\\ -x+4y \\\\ -3x-5y\\end{aligned}]$ be a linear transformation. Determine the value of $k$ for which the vector $v=[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ -4 \\\\ k\\end{aligned}]$ belongs to $\\text{Im}(T).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071327/state.json" "file_sha256" "6338a411ddc682cdb4cfa8c78c2417d95adbad870b870077447b1e6aa0e80ae0"} {"question" "q-38615" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The vector $v$ belongs to $\\text{Im}(T)$ if there exists a vector $u=[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]$ such that $T(u)=v.$ \n\nTherefore, we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}-2x+y \\\\ -x+4y \\\\ -3x-5y\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ -4 \\\\ k\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-2x+y=6 \\\\ -x+4y=-4 \\\\ -3x-5y=k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get $x=4+4y$, which we substitute into the first equation to get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2x+y & =6 \\\\ -2(4+4y)+y & =6 \\\\ -7y & =14 \\\\ y & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =4+4y \\\\  & =4+4(-2) \\\\  & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $x=-4$ and $y=-2$ into the third equation and obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3x-5y & =k \\\\ -3(-4)-5(-2) & =k \\\\ k & =22.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $v\\in \\text{Im}(T)$ when $k=22.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071327/state.json" "file_sha256" "6338a411ddc682cdb4cfa8c78c2417d95adbad870b870077447b1e6aa0e80ae0"} {"question" "q-38615" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071327/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "a915ae1620aba781bc802cb39da8c1826f600fa88b93890bbd4cf99ac6ab65e2"} {"question" "q-38615" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071327/state.json" "file_sha256" "6338a411ddc682cdb4cfa8c78c2417d95adbad870b870077447b1e6aa0e80ae0"} {"question" "q-38615" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-12"] ["math" "-2"] ["math" "2"] ["math" "20"] ["math" "22"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071327/state.json" "file_sha256" "6338a411ddc682cdb4cfa8c78c2417d95adbad870b870077447b1e6aa0e80ae0"} {"question" "q-38615" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "22"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071327/state.json" "file_sha256" "6338a411ddc682cdb4cfa8c78c2417d95adbad870b870077447b1e6aa0e80ae0"} {"question" "q-80818" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $T:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}3x+y \\\\ x+2y \\\\ -2x+7y\\end{aligned}]$ be a linear transformation. Determine the value of $k$ for which the vector $v=[\\begin{aligned}9 \\\\ 8 \\\\ k\\end{aligned}]$ belongs to $\\text{Im}(T).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071327/state.json" "file_sha256" "6338a411ddc682cdb4cfa8c78c2417d95adbad870b870077447b1e6aa0e80ae0"} {"question" "q-80818" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The vector $v$ belongs to $\\text{Im}(T)$ if there exists a vector $u=[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]$ such that $T(u)=v.$ \n\nTherefore, we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}3x+y \\\\ x+2y \\\\ -2x+7y\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}9 \\\\ 8 \\\\ k\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}3x+y=9 \\\\ x+2y=8 \\\\ -2x+7y=k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get $x=8-2y$, which we substitute into the first equation to get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x+y & =9 \\\\ 3(8-2y)+y & =9 \\\\ -5y & =-15 \\\\ y & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =8-2y \\\\  & =8-2(3) \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $x=2$ and $y=3$ into the third equation and obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2x+7y & =k \\\\ -2(2)+7(3) & =k \\\\ k & =17.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $v\\in \\text{Im}(T)$ when $k=17.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071327/state.json" "file_sha256" "6338a411ddc682cdb4cfa8c78c2417d95adbad870b870077447b1e6aa0e80ae0"} {"question" "q-80818" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071327/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "a915ae1620aba781bc802cb39da8c1826f600fa88b93890bbd4cf99ac6ab65e2"} {"question" "q-80818" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071327/state.json" "file_sha256" "6338a411ddc682cdb4cfa8c78c2417d95adbad870b870077447b1e6aa0e80ae0"} {"question" "q-80818" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "17"] ["math" "21"] ["math" "25"] ["math" "4"] ["math" "7"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071327/state.json" "file_sha256" "6338a411ddc682cdb4cfa8c78c2417d95adbad870b870077447b1e6aa0e80ae0"} {"question" "q-80818" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "17"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071327/state.json" "file_sha256" "6338a411ddc682cdb4cfa8c78c2417d95adbad870b870077447b1e6aa0e80ae0"} {"question" "q-140068" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $T=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -4 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -3 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 4 & 0\\end{aligned}]$ be the standard matrix of the linear transformation $T.$ Determine the value of $k$ for which the vector $v=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 7 \\\\ k\\end{aligned}]$  belongs to $\\text{Im}(T).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071327/state.json" "file_sha256" "6338a411ddc682cdb4cfa8c78c2417d95adbad870b870077447b1e6aa0e80ae0"} {"question" "q-140068" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The vector $v$ belongs to $\\text{Im}(T)$ if there exists a vector $u=[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]$ such that $T(u)=v.$\n\nTherefore, we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T\\cdot u & =v \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}2 & -4 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -3 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 4 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 7 \\\\ k\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}2x-4y \\\\ x-3y \\\\ -x+4y\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 7 \\\\ k\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2x-4y=2 \\\\ x-3y=7 \\\\ -x+4y=k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get $x=7+3y$, which we substitute into the first equation to get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-4y & =2 \\\\ 2(7+3y)-4y & =2 \\\\ 2y & =-12 \\\\ y & =-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =7+3y \\\\  & =7+3(-6) \\\\  & =-11.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $x=-11$ and $y=-6$ into the third equation and obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-x+4y & =k \\\\ -(-11)+4(-6) & =k \\\\ k & =-13.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $v\\in \\text{Im}(T)$ when $k=-13.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071327/state.json" "file_sha256" "6338a411ddc682cdb4cfa8c78c2417d95adbad870b870077447b1e6aa0e80ae0"} {"question" "q-140068" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071327/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "a915ae1620aba781bc802cb39da8c1826f600fa88b93890bbd4cf99ac6ab65e2"} {"question" "q-140068" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071327/state.json" "file_sha256" "6338a411ddc682cdb4cfa8c78c2417d95adbad870b870077447b1e6aa0e80ae0"} {"question" "q-140068" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-13"] ["math" "-24"] ["math" "11"] ["math" "14"] ["math" "24"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071327/state.json" "file_sha256" "6338a411ddc682cdb4cfa8c78c2417d95adbad870b870077447b1e6aa0e80ae0"} {"question" "q-140068" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-13"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071327/state.json" "file_sha256" "6338a411ddc682cdb4cfa8c78c2417d95adbad870b870077447b1e6aa0e80ae0"} {"question" "q-38627" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $T=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -8 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -4 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 10 & 0\\end{aligned}]$ be the standard matrix of the linear transformation $T.$ Determine the value of $k$ for which the vector $v=[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 8 \\\\ k\\end{aligned}]$ belongs to $\\text{Im}(T).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071327/state.json" "file_sha256" "6338a411ddc682cdb4cfa8c78c2417d95adbad870b870077447b1e6aa0e80ae0"} {"question" "q-38627" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The vector $v$ belongs to $\\text{Im}(T)$ if there exists a vector $u=[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]$ such that $T(u)=v.$\n\nTherefore, we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T\\cdot u & =v \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}3 & -8 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -4 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 10 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 8 \\\\ k\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}3x-8y \\\\ 2x-4y \\\\ -2x+10y\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 8 \\\\ k\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}3x-8y=6 \\\\ 2x-4y=8 \\\\ -2x+10y=k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get $x=4+2y$, which we substitute into the first equation to get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x-8y & =6 \\\\ 3(4+2y)-8y & =6 \\\\ -2y & =-6 \\\\ y & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =4+2y \\\\  & =4+2(3) \\\\  & =10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $x=10$ and $y=3$ into the third equation and obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2x+10y & =k \\\\ -2(10)+10(3) & =k \\\\ k & =10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $v\\in \\text{Im}(T)$ when $k=10.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071327/state.json" "file_sha256" "6338a411ddc682cdb4cfa8c78c2417d95adbad870b870077447b1e6aa0e80ae0"} {"question" "q-38627" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071327/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "a915ae1620aba781bc802cb39da8c1826f600fa88b93890bbd4cf99ac6ab65e2"} {"question" "q-38627" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071327/state.json" "file_sha256" "6338a411ddc682cdb4cfa8c78c2417d95adbad870b870077447b1e6aa0e80ae0"} {"question" "q-38627" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-4"] ["math" "-5"] ["math" "1"] ["math" "10"] ["math" "4"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071327/state.json" "file_sha256" "6338a411ddc682cdb4cfa8c78c2417d95adbad870b870077447b1e6aa0e80ae0"} {"question" "q-38627" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "10"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071327/state.json" "file_sha256" "6338a411ddc682cdb4cfa8c78c2417d95adbad870b870077447b1e6aa0e80ae0"} {"question" "q-81242" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $T=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]$ be the standard matrix of the linear transformation $T.$ Determine the value of $k$ for which the vector $v=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ k \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]$ belongs to $\\text{Im}(T).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071327/state.json" "file_sha256" "6338a411ddc682cdb4cfa8c78c2417d95adbad870b870077447b1e6aa0e80ae0"} {"question" "q-81242" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The vector $v$ belongs to $\\text{Im}(T)$ if there exists a vector $u=[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]$ such that $T(u)=v.$\n\nTherefore, we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T\\cdot u & =v \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}-3 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ k \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}-3x+2y \\\\ -2y \\\\ -5x+y\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ k \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-3x+2y=5 \\\\ -2y=k \\\\ -5x+y=-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the third equation, we get $y=5x-1$, which we substitute into the first equation to get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3x+2y & =5 \\\\ -3x+2(5x-1) & =5 \\\\ 7x & =7 \\\\ x & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =5x-1 \\\\  & =5(1)-1 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $y=4$ into the second equation and obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2y & =k \\\\ -2(4) & =k \\\\ k & =-8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $v\\in \\text{Im}(T)$ when $k=-8.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071327/state.json" "file_sha256" "6338a411ddc682cdb4cfa8c78c2417d95adbad870b870077447b1e6aa0e80ae0"} {"question" "q-81242" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071327/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "a915ae1620aba781bc802cb39da8c1826f600fa88b93890bbd4cf99ac6ab65e2"} {"question" "q-81242" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071327/state.json" "file_sha256" "6338a411ddc682cdb4cfa8c78c2417d95adbad870b870077447b1e6aa0e80ae0"} {"question" "q-81242" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-1"] ["math" "-2"] ["math" "-4"] ["math" "-8"] ["math" "1"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071327/state.json" "file_sha256" "6338a411ddc682cdb4cfa8c78c2417d95adbad870b870077447b1e6aa0e80ae0"} {"question" "q-81242" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-8"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071327/state.json" "file_sha256" "6338a411ddc682cdb4cfa8c78c2417d95adbad870b870077447b1e6aa0e80ae0"} {"question" "q-38625" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The standard matrix $T$ of a linear transformation $T$ and the vector $b\\in {R}^{3}$ are given below. Determine if there exists a vector $x\\in {R}^{3}$ such that $T(x)=b.$\nIn the affirmative case, find the value of ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2},$ where ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3}$ are the components of $x.$  \n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 2 & 4 \\\\ -2 & -4 & 4\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ -28 \\\\ 26\\end{aligned}]\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071327/state.json" "file_sha256" "6338a411ddc682cdb4cfa8c78c2417d95adbad870b870077447b1e6aa0e80ae0"} {"question" "q-38625" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since $T$ is the standard matix of $T,$ we have $\\text{Im}(T)=\\text{Col}(T).$ In order to determine whether $b$ lies in $\\text{Im}(T),$ we need to check if the equation $Tx=b$ is consistent. \n\nSo, we find the row echelon form of the augmented matrix $[T\\,|\\,b],$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[T\\,|\\,b] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 2 & -5 \\\\ 4 & 2 & 4 & -28 \\\\ -2 & -4 & 4 & 26\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-4){R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+2{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 2 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -4 & -8 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 8 & 16\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+2{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 2 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -4 & -8 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has two pivot columns (the $1$st and the $2$nd). Hence ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. Now, from the second equation, we get \n\n$$\n2{x}_{2}-4{x}_{3}=-8\\,⟹\\,{x}_{2}=-4+2{x}_{3}.\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation we obtain \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}+2{x}_{3}=-5\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=-5-2{x}_{3}.\n$$\n\nAs a result, the general solution is given by \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-5-2{x}_{3} \\\\ -4+2{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{3}=1$, we have\n\n$$\nx=[\\begin{aligned}-7 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nwhere $T(x)=b.$\n\nFinally, ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=-7+(-2)=-9.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071327/state.json" "file_sha256" "6338a411ddc682cdb4cfa8c78c2417d95adbad870b870077447b1e6aa0e80ae0"} {"question" "q-38625" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071327/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "a915ae1620aba781bc802cb39da8c1826f600fa88b93890bbd4cf99ac6ab65e2"} {"question" "q-38625" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071327/state.json" "file_sha256" "6338a411ddc682cdb4cfa8c78c2417d95adbad870b870077447b1e6aa0e80ae0"} {"question" "q-38625" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-3"] ["math" "-7"] ["math" "-9"] ["math" "\\frac{5}{4}"] ["math" "b\\notin \\text{Im}(T)"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071327/state.json" "file_sha256" "6338a411ddc682cdb4cfa8c78c2417d95adbad870b870077447b1e6aa0e80ae0"} {"question" "q-38625" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-9"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071327/state.json" "file_sha256" "6338a411ddc682cdb4cfa8c78c2417d95adbad870b870077447b1e6aa0e80ae0"} {"question" "q-38629" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The linear transformation $T$ and the vector $b\\in {R}^{4}$ are given below. Determine if there exists a vector $x\\in {R}^{4}$ such that $T(x)=b.$\nIn the affirmative case, find the value of $w-x+y,$ where $w,x,y,z$ are the components of $x.$  \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}w \\\\ x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}w-3x-2z \\\\ 2x-2w-4y \\\\ 3w-y-10x-7z \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071327/state.json" "file_sha256" "6338a411ddc682cdb4cfa8c78c2417d95adbad870b870077447b1e6aa0e80ae0"} {"question" "q-38629" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we write down the standard matrix of $T:$\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 0 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 2 & -4 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -10 & -1 & -7 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nSince $T$ is the standard matix of $T,$ we have $\\text{Im}(T)=\\text{Col}(T).$ In order to determine whether $b$ lies in $\\text{Im}(T),$ we need to check if the equation $Tx=b$ is consistent. \n\nSo, let's find the row echelon form of the augmented matrix $[T\\,|\\,b]$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[T\\,|\\,b] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 0 & -2 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 2 & -4 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 3 & -10 & -1 & -7 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-3){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 0 & -2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -4 & -4 & -4 & 8 \\\\ 0 & -1 & -1 & -1 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-\\frac{1}{4}){R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 0 & -2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -4 & -4 & -4 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & -8 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the third equation, we get the false statement $0=-8.$ Therefore, the system is inconsistent, and we conclude that  $b\\notin \\text{Im}(T).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071327/state.json" "file_sha256" "6338a411ddc682cdb4cfa8c78c2417d95adbad870b870077447b1e6aa0e80ae0"} {"question" "q-38629" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071327/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "a915ae1620aba781bc802cb39da8c1826f600fa88b93890bbd4cf99ac6ab65e2"} {"question" "q-38629" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071327/state.json" "file_sha256" "6338a411ddc682cdb4cfa8c78c2417d95adbad870b870077447b1e6aa0e80ae0"} {"question" "q-38629" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-3"] ["math" "-\\frac{3}{5}"] ["math" "11"] ["math" "\\frac{4}{5}"] ["math" "b\\notin \\text{Im}(T)"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071327/state.json" "file_sha256" "6338a411ddc682cdb4cfa8c78c2417d95adbad870b870077447b1e6aa0e80ae0"} {"question" "q-38629" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "b\\notin \\text{Im}(T)"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071327/state.json" "file_sha256" "6338a411ddc682cdb4cfa8c78c2417d95adbad870b870077447b1e6aa0e80ae0"} {"question" "q-38001" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The standard matrix $T$ of a linear transformation $T$ and the vector $b\\in {R}^{3}$ are given below. Determine if there exists a vector $x\\in {R}^{3}$ such that $T(x)=b.$\nIn the affirmative case, find the value of ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2},$ where ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3}$ are the components of $x.$  \n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -5 & 3 \\\\ 4 & -1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -3\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}9 \\\\ -7 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071327/state.json" "file_sha256" "6338a411ddc682cdb4cfa8c78c2417d95adbad870b870077447b1e6aa0e80ae0"} {"question" "q-38001" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since $T$ is the standard matix of $T,$ we have $\\text{Im}(T)=\\text{Col}(T).$ In order to determine whether $b$ lies in $\\text{Im}(T),$ we need to check if the equation $Tx=b$ is consistent. \n\nSo, we find the row echelon form of the augmented matrix $[T\\,|\\,b],$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[T\\,|\\,b] & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -5 & 3 & 9 \\\\ 4 & -1 & 5 & -7 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -3 & -3\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -5 & 3 & 9 \\\\ 0 & -11 & 11 & 11 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -3 & -3\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+\\frac{3}{11}{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -5 & 3 & 9 \\\\ 0 & -11 & 11 & 11 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has two pivot columns (the $1$st and the $2$nd). Hence, ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. \nNow, from the second equation, we get \n\n$$\n-11{x}_{2}+11{x}_{3}=11\\,⟹\\,{x}_{2}=-1+{x}_{3}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation gives \n\n$$\n-2{x}_{1}-5(-1+{x}_{3})+3{x}_{3}=9\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=-2-{x}_{3}.\n$$\n\nAs a result, the general solution is given by \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-2-{x}_{3} \\\\ -1+{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{3}=0,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nwhere $T(x)=b.$\n\nFinally, ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=-2-1=-3.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071327/state.json" "file_sha256" "6338a411ddc682cdb4cfa8c78c2417d95adbad870b870077447b1e6aa0e80ae0"} {"question" "q-38001" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071327/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "a915ae1620aba781bc802cb39da8c1826f600fa88b93890bbd4cf99ac6ab65e2"} {"question" "q-38001" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071327/state.json" "file_sha256" "6338a411ddc682cdb4cfa8c78c2417d95adbad870b870077447b1e6aa0e80ae0"} {"question" "q-38001" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-3"] ["math" "1"] ["math" "4"] ["math" "\\frac{2}{3}"] ["math" "b\\notin \\text{Im}(T)"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071327/state.json" "file_sha256" "6338a411ddc682cdb4cfa8c78c2417d95adbad870b870077447b1e6aa0e80ae0"} {"question" "q-38001" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-3"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071327/state.json" "file_sha256" "6338a411ddc682cdb4cfa8c78c2417d95adbad870b870077447b1e6aa0e80ae0"} {"question" "q-140075" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The linear transformation $T$ and the vector $b\\in {R}^{4}$ are given below. Determine if there exists a vector $x\\in {R}^{4}$ such that $T(x)=b.$\nIn the affirmative case, find the value of ${x}_{1}-{x}_{3},$ where ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3},{x}_{4}$ are the components of $x.$  \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{4}\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}-{x}_{4} \\\\ -{x}_{2}+2{x}_{3}+{x}_{4} \\\\ 2{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}+{x}_{4} \\\\ -11{x}_{3}-2{x}_{4}\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071327/state.json" "file_sha256" "6338a411ddc682cdb4cfa8c78c2417d95adbad870b870077447b1e6aa0e80ae0"} {"question" "q-140075" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we write down the standard matrix of $T:$\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -11 & -2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nSince $T$ is the standard matix of $T,$ we have $\\text{Im}(T)=\\text{Col}(T).$ In order to determine whether $b$ lies in $\\text{Im}(T),$ we need to check if the equation $Tx=b$ is consistent. \n\nSo, let's find the row echelon form of the augmented matrix $[T\\,|\\,b]$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[T\\,|\\,b] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 2 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 1 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -11 & -2 & 5\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-2){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 2 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -5 & -1 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -11 & -2 & 5\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-5){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 2 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -11 & -2 & 11 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -11 & -2 & 5\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{4}:={R}_{4}+(-1){R}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 2 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -11 & -2 & 11 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & -6\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the third equation, we get the false statement $0=-6.$ Therefore, the system is inconsistent, and we conclude that  $b\\notin \\text{Im}(T).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071327/state.json" "file_sha256" "6338a411ddc682cdb4cfa8c78c2417d95adbad870b870077447b1e6aa0e80ae0"} {"question" "q-140075" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071327/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "a915ae1620aba781bc802cb39da8c1826f600fa88b93890bbd4cf99ac6ab65e2"} {"question" "q-140075" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071327/state.json" "file_sha256" "6338a411ddc682cdb4cfa8c78c2417d95adbad870b870077447b1e6aa0e80ae0"} {"question" "q-140075" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2"] ["math" "3"] ["math" "4"] ["math" "5"] ["math" "b\\notin \\text{Im}(T)"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071327/state.json" "file_sha256" "6338a411ddc682cdb4cfa8c78c2417d95adbad870b870077447b1e6aa0e80ae0"} {"question" "q-140075" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "b\\notin \\text{Im}(T)"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071327/state.json" "file_sha256" "6338a411ddc682cdb4cfa8c78c2417d95adbad870b870077447b1e6aa0e80ae0"} {"question" "q-38007" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The linear transformation $T$ and the vector $b\\in {R}^{4}$ are given below. Determine if there exists a vector $x\\in {R}^{4}$ such that $T(x)=b.$\nIn the affirmative case, find the value of ${x}_{1}-{x}_{3},$ where ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3},{x}_{4}$ are the components of $x.$  \n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{4}\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}-{x}_{4} \\\\ 2{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}+3{x}_{3}+4{x}_{4} \\\\ 2{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}-{x}_{3}-3{x}_{4} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 6 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071327/state.json" "file_sha256" "6338a411ddc682cdb4cfa8c78c2417d95adbad870b870077447b1e6aa0e80ae0"} {"question" "q-38007" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we write down the standard matrix of $T:$\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 3 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 1 & -1 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nSince $T$ is the standard matix of $T,$ we have $\\text{Im}(T)=\\text{Col}(T).$ In order to determine whether $b$ lies in $\\text{Im}(T),$ we need to check if the equation $Tx=b$ is consistent. \n\nSo, let's find the row echelon form of the augmented matrix $[T\\,|\\,b]$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[T\\,|\\,b] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 0 & -1 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 3 & 4 & 6 \\\\ 2 & 1 & -1 & -3 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-2){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 0 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 3 & 3 & 9 \\\\ 0 & -1 & -1 & -1 & -9 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}={R}_{3}+\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 0 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 3 & 3 & 9 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the third equation, we get the false statement $0=-6.$ Therefore, the system is inconsistent, and we conclude that  $b\\notin \\text{Im}(T).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071327/state.json" "file_sha256" "6338a411ddc682cdb4cfa8c78c2417d95adbad870b870077447b1e6aa0e80ae0"} {"question" "q-38007" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071327/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "a915ae1620aba781bc802cb39da8c1826f600fa88b93890bbd4cf99ac6ab65e2"} {"question" "q-38007" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071327/state.json" "file_sha256" "6338a411ddc682cdb4cfa8c78c2417d95adbad870b870077447b1e6aa0e80ae0"} {"question" "q-38007" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-\\frac{3}{4}"] ["math" "3"] ["math" "5"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{5}"] ["math" "b\\notin \\text{Im}(T)"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071327/state.json" "file_sha256" "6338a411ddc682cdb4cfa8c78c2417d95adbad870b870077447b1e6aa0e80ae0"} {"question" "q-38007" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "b\\notin \\text{Im}(T)"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071327/state.json" "file_sha256" "6338a411ddc682cdb4cfa8c78c2417d95adbad870b870077447b1e6aa0e80ae0"} {"question" "q-37939" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given the transformation $T:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}x+y+z \\\\ z+y-x \\\\ z-2y\\end{aligned}],$ find its rank." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071327/state.json" "file_sha256" "6338a411ddc682cdb4cfa8c78c2417d95adbad870b870077447b1e6aa0e80ae0"} {"question" "q-37939" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, the standard matrix of $T$ is given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nRecall that $\\text{rank}(T)=\\text{dim}(\\text{Im}(T))=\\text{rank}(T).$ \n\nSo, we need to find the rank of the matrix $T.$ We reduce $T$ to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 1\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, since $T$ has $3$ pivot columns, we have that \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(T)=\\text{rank}(T)=3.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071327/state.json" "file_sha256" "6338a411ddc682cdb4cfa8c78c2417d95adbad870b870077447b1e6aa0e80ae0"} {"question" "q-37939" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071327/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "a915ae1620aba781bc802cb39da8c1826f600fa88b93890bbd4cf99ac6ab65e2"} {"question" "q-37939" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071327/state.json" "file_sha256" "6338a411ddc682cdb4cfa8c78c2417d95adbad870b870077447b1e6aa0e80ae0"} {"question" "q-37939" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "0"] ["math" "1"] ["math" "2"] ["math" "3"] ["math" "4"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071327/state.json" "file_sha256" "6338a411ddc682cdb4cfa8c78c2417d95adbad870b870077447b1e6aa0e80ae0"} {"question" "q-37939" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "3"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071327/state.json" "file_sha256" "6338a411ddc682cdb4cfa8c78c2417d95adbad870b870077447b1e6aa0e80ae0"} {"question" "q-40210" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given the transformation $T:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}x+2y \\\\ y-x+6z \\\\ 8z-x+3y\\end{aligned}],$ find its rank." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071327/state.json" "file_sha256" "6338a411ddc682cdb4cfa8c78c2417d95adbad870b870077447b1e6aa0e80ae0"} {"question" "q-40210" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, the standard matrix of $T$ is given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 6 \\\\ -1 & 3 & 8\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nRecall that $\\text{rank}(T)=\\text{dim}(\\text{Im}(T))=\\text{rank}(T).$ \n\nSo, we need to find the rank of the matrix $T.$ We reduce $T$ to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 6 \\\\ -1 & 3 & 8\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 8\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-\\frac{5}{3}){R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, since $T$ has $3$ pivot columns, we have that \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(T)=\\text{rank}(T)=3.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071327/state.json" "file_sha256" "6338a411ddc682cdb4cfa8c78c2417d95adbad870b870077447b1e6aa0e80ae0"} {"question" "q-40210" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071327/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "a915ae1620aba781bc802cb39da8c1826f600fa88b93890bbd4cf99ac6ab65e2"} {"question" "q-40210" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071327/state.json" "file_sha256" "6338a411ddc682cdb4cfa8c78c2417d95adbad870b870077447b1e6aa0e80ae0"} {"question" "q-40210" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "0"] ["math" "1"] ["math" "2"] ["math" "3"] ["math" "4"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071327/state.json" "file_sha256" "6338a411ddc682cdb4cfa8c78c2417d95adbad870b870077447b1e6aa0e80ae0"} {"question" "q-40210" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "3"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071327/state.json" "file_sha256" "6338a411ddc682cdb4cfa8c78c2417d95adbad870b870077447b1e6aa0e80ae0"} {"question" "q-38006" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given the transformation $T:[\\begin{aligned}w \\\\ x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}w-x+2y-z \\\\ x-y-z \\\\ 4w-y+z \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],$ find its rank." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071327/state.json" "file_sha256" "6338a411ddc682cdb4cfa8c78c2417d95adbad870b870077447b1e6aa0e80ae0"} {"question" "q-38006" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, the standard matrix of $T$ is given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 4 & 0 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nRecall that $\\text{rank}(T)=\\text{dim}(\\text{Im}(T))=\\text{rank}(T).$ \n\nSo, we need to find the rank of the matrix $T.$ We reduce $T$ to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 4 & 0 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}-4{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 4 & -9 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}-4{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -5 & 9 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, since $T$ has $3$ pivot columns, we have that \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(T)=\\text{rank}(T)=3.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071327/state.json" "file_sha256" "6338a411ddc682cdb4cfa8c78c2417d95adbad870b870077447b1e6aa0e80ae0"} {"question" "q-38006" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate