form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 & 4 \\\\ 9 & -8 & 10 \\\\ -3 & 2 & -1\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, since $T$ has $3$ pivot column, we have that \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(T)=\\text{rank}(T)=3.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071327/state.json" "file_sha256" "6338a411ddc682cdb4cfa8c78c2417d95adbad870b870077447b1e6aa0e80ae0"} {"question" "q-81237" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071327/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "a915ae1620aba781bc802cb39da8c1826f600fa88b93890bbd4cf99ac6ab65e2"} {"question" "q-81237" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071327/state.json" "file_sha256" "6338a411ddc682cdb4cfa8c78c2417d95adbad870b870077447b1e6aa0e80ae0"} {"question" "q-81237" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "0"] ["math" "1"] ["math" "2"] ["math" "3"] ["math" "4"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071327/state.json" "file_sha256" "6338a411ddc682cdb4cfa8c78c2417d95adbad870b870077447b1e6aa0e80ae0"} {"question" "q-81237" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "3"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071327/state.json" "file_sha256" "6338a411ddc682cdb4cfa8c78c2417d95adbad870b870077447b1e6aa0e80ae0"} {"question" "q-37937" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "If $T=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 14 & -14 \\\\ 2 & 3 & -5\\end{aligned}]$ is the standard matrix of the linear transformation $T$, then $\\text{rank}(T)=$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071327/state.json" "file_sha256" "6338a411ddc682cdb4cfa8c78c2417d95adbad870b870077447b1e6aa0e80ae0"} {"question" "q-37937" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall that $\\text{rank}(T)=\\text{dim}(\\text{Im}(T))=\\text{rank}(T).$ \n\nSo, we need to find the rank of the matrix $T.$ We reduce $T$ to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 14 & -14 \\\\ 2 & 3 & -5\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-2){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 14 & -14 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 3\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+\\frac{3}{14}{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 14 & -14 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, since $T$ has $2$ pivot columns, we have that \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(T)=\\text{rank}(T)=2.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071327/state.json" "file_sha256" "6338a411ddc682cdb4cfa8c78c2417d95adbad870b870077447b1e6aa0e80ae0"} {"question" "q-37937" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071327/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "a915ae1620aba781bc802cb39da8c1826f600fa88b93890bbd4cf99ac6ab65e2"} {"question" "q-37937" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071327/state.json" "file_sha256" "6338a411ddc682cdb4cfa8c78c2417d95adbad870b870077447b1e6aa0e80ae0"} {"question" "q-37937" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "0"] ["math" "1"] ["math" "2"] ["math" "3"] ["math" "4"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071327/state.json" "file_sha256" "6338a411ddc682cdb4cfa8c78c2417d95adbad870b870077447b1e6aa0e80ae0"} {"question" "q-37937" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071327/state.json" "file_sha256" "6338a411ddc682cdb4cfa8c78c2417d95adbad870b870077447b1e6aa0e80ae0"} {"question" "e-5619" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071327/assets/1c8969401a7c812e945fdc0b2f1774f6cd1d1d9c35269b2b20ea8165449d427f.png) be a linear transformation. Determine the value of $k$ for which the vector ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071327/assets/05f7384dd957b2616433104bfefbb8b3e7aaa619dbb1dff94fec43c8a99e6c3c.png) belongs to $\\textrm{Im}(\\mathbf{T}).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071327/example-5619.json" "file_sha256" "7bcadf09fe76433f8aa0237b6a817a97b8b81f6600b356a73b3b75fa18a98037"} {"question" "e-5619" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The vector $\\mathbf{v}$ belongs to $\\textrm{Im}(\\mathbf{T})$ if there exists a vector ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071327/assets/be82bf02cdfe6a50557b312ba78708463248f844f81016df2cda41c6cfbb6c1a.png) such that $\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{u}) = \\mathbf{v}.$ \n\nTherefore, we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x+y \\\\ -x+y \\\\ 2y\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ k \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x+y=4 \\\\ -x+y=k \\\\ 2y=8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the third equation, we get $y=4,$ which we substitute into the first equation to get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+y & =4 \\\\ x+4 & =4 \\\\ x & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $x=0$ and $y=4$ into the third equation and obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-x+y & =k \\\\ 0+4 & =k \\\\ k & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\mathbf{v}\\in\\textrm{Im}(\\mathbf{T})$ when $k=4.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071327/example-5619.json" "file_sha256" "7bcadf09fe76433f8aa0237b6a817a97b8b81f6600b356a73b3b75fa18a98037"} {"question" "e-5442" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The standard matrix $T$ of a linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ and the vector $\\mathbf{b} \\in \\mathbb{R}^3$ are given below. Determine if there exists a vector $\\mathbf{x} \\in \\mathbb{R}^3$ such that $\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{x})=\\mathbf{b}.$ In the affirmative case, find such a vector.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071327/assets/36675dd67326016b3083bfb87ec93ae427a74879ce41007a4cadf11f6cfca80d.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071327/example-5442.json" "file_sha256" "ee23c24903d8846e851e289ed66cb106994ff11947de6b84e3be0fb9c8a9e4c8"} {"question" "e-5442" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since $T$ is the standard matix of $\\mathbf{T},$ we have $\\textrm{Im}(\\mathbf{T}) = \\textrm{Col}(T).$ In order to determine whether $\\mathbf{b}$ lies in $\\textrm{Im}(\\mathbf{T}),$ we need to check if the equation $T\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}$ is consistent. \n\nSo, we find the row echelon form of the augmented matrix $[T \\,|\\, \\mathbf{b}],$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[T\\,|\\,b] & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 & 2 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 4 & -10 & 26 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 8 & -4\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 & 2 & 6 \\\\ 0 & -8 & -16 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 8 & -4\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 & 2 & 6 \\\\ 0 & -8 & -16 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has a zero row, and so the system is consistent. We have two pivot columns (the $1$st and the $2$nd). So, $x_3$ is a free variable. \nNow, from the second equation, we get \n\n$$\n-8x_2-16x_3 = 8\n\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\nx_2=-1-2x_3.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation gives \n\n$$\n2x_1+4(-1-2x_3)+2x_3 = 6\n\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\nx_1=5+3x_3.\n$$\n\nAs a result, the general solution is given by \n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071327/assets/72aba565539bdf6cee541e7495665a5bd422f8e7c5693ab68794c0a284c42e8d.png)\n\nSetting $x_3=0,$ we have\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071327/assets/9322232405d24c433a3870b3a29d8deb8c85e01e5a88eabcc48e51be75c21e9d.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071327/example-5442.json" "file_sha256" "ee23c24903d8846e851e289ed66cb106994ff11947de6b84e3be0fb9c8a9e4c8"} {"question" "e-5443" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "If ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071327/assets/71adf512017629aa4cfc010c18bc840ea63459818bae4ebc0b7dba59193c0d00.png) is the standard matrix of the linear transformation $\\mathbf{T},$ then what is $\\text{rank}(\\mathbf{T})?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071327/example-5443.json" "file_sha256" "3cfa888808b09bd364237b57ea288d0e59fa48df72dface922a8e1ba28d10b30"} {"question" "e-5443" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "In general, $\\text{rank}(\\mathbf{T}) = \\text{dim}(\\text{Im}(\\mathbf{T})) = \\text{rank}(T).$ \n\nSo, we need to find the rank of the matrix $T.$ We reduce $T$ to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 & -1 & -2 \\\\ -6 & 5 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 1 & 5 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+3{R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-2){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 & -1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -4 & -4 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 7 & 7 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+\\frac{7}{4}{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 & -1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -4 & -4 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, since $T$ has $2$ pivot columns, we have that \n\n$$\n\\textrm{rank}(\\mathbf{T})=\\textrm{rank}(T)=2.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071327/example-5443.json" "file_sha256" "3cfa888808b09bd364237b57ea288d0e59fa48df72dface922a8e1ba28d10b30"} {"question" "q-38007" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "b\\notin \\text{Im}(T)"] "category" "reviewed_ma_solution" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071327/answer-source-reviews.json" "file_sha256" "fc00ec135be06bd0aaed77697de98d8b85518958e66967ddb5268c7bea263a34" "reviewer_session" "01a118cc-89a0-7c90-9221-d6599ae13bca" "rationale" "Derived from the original transformation: its first three coefficient rows satisfy R3=(5/3)R1-(1/3)R2. Any vector in its image must therefore satisfy b3=(5/3)b1-(1/3)b2. For the given b=(3,6,-3,0), the right side is 3, whereas b3=-3. Thus T(x)=b is inconsistent and b is not in Im(T). This agrees with the authentic worked solution's contradiction 0=-6, original option c (zero-based index 2), and saved Correct grading. The stored Unicode nonmembership symbol and captured TeX notation have identical mathematical meaning." "bindings" {"problem" "987fc4777a75397e899b28810724d795d746a2e210c1d7991a9846ad98818d14" "worked_solution" "8dd401be72cbee91f6e53700f3c6ba976f0229332ce605069a5ea47b0d080ee5" "original_field" "d05a45b45e554735c60197ccf07f114f3f84ad377f0574a9b7249fde655de4b2" "reviewed_field" "e20ecd33daff26eafe15860bf5c4eed3930bd0e18c6b77080694575c4c105636" "raw_widget" "e63617a78c9c82d2e4ae3830d1c0b80c845bc37ec49d4ac9ad55e84236b256cc"} "evidence_files" [{"path" "q-38007-before.png" "sha256" "59a7dc61305c494d605c6e7fe620a9dafb7513fb08ceb00e4dc6f2151dba3fca"} {"path" "q-38007-before.json" "sha256" "99a847894857d287c7b97bad6e9dc51b8301278293ed501a2db62c1ddb2fee36"} {"path" "history-q-38007.png" "sha256" "00036bd572c5ec5cd532e9fc87e15b881d2ca4f428a2975efc91d68b57f5db7b"} {"path" "q-38007-after.json" "sha256" "7c78308c896611ff572741953df29c35b314ead662f420770cef94a1f1f33765"}]} {"question" "q-38007" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-\\frac{3}{4}"] ["math" "3"] ["math" "5"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{5}"] ["math" "b\\notin \\text{Im}(T)"]] "category" "reviewed_ma_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071327/answer-source-reviews.json" "file_sha256" "fc00ec135be06bd0aaed77697de98d8b85518958e66967ddb5268c7bea263a34" "rationale" "Derived from the original transformation: its first three coefficient rows satisfy R3=(5/3)R1-(1/3)R2. Any vector in its image must therefore satisfy b3=(5/3)b1-(1/3)b2. For the given b=(3,6,-3,0), the right side is 3, whereas b3=-3. Thus T(x)=b is inconsistent and b is not in Im(T). This agrees with the authentic worked solution's contradiction 0=-6, original option c (zero-based index 2), and saved Correct grading. The stored Unicode nonmembership symbol and captured TeX notation have identical mathematical meaning."}] "usage" {"q-38627" [] "e-5619" [] "q-38615" [] "e-5442" [] "q-38001" [] "q-38007" [] "q-40210" [] "e-5443" []} "basis" 2545 "retractions" [[17592186125048 :question/problem "Let $T: \\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}-2x + y \\\\ -x + 4y \\\\ -3x - 5y\\end{bmatrix}$ be a linear transformation. Determine the value of $k$ for which the vector $v = \\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ -4 \\\\ k\\end{bmatrix}$ belongs to $Im(T)$."] [17592186125049 :question/problem "Let $T = \\begin{bmatrix}3 & -8 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -4 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 10 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$ be the standard matrix of the linear transformation $T$. Determine the value of $k$ for which the vector $v = \\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 8 \\\\ k\\end{bmatrix}$ belongs to $Im(T)$."] [17592186125031 :question/problem "The standard matrix $T$ of a linear transformation $T$ and the vector $b ∈ R^{3}$ are given below. Determine if there exists a vector $x ∈ R^{3}$ such that $T(x) = b$.\nIn the affirmative case, find the value of $x_{1} + x_{2}$, where $x_{1}, x_{2}, x_{3}$ are the components of $x$.\n\n$T = \\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -5 & 3 \\\\ 4 & -1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -3\\end{bmatrix}, b = \\begin{bmatrix}9 \\\\ -7 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}$"] [17592186125031 :question/answer-fields 17592186123208] [17592186125033 :question/problem "The linear transformation $T$ and the vector $b ∈ R^{4}$ are given below. Determine if there exists a vector $x ∈ R^{4}$ such that $T(x) = b$.\nIn the affirmative case, find the value of $x_{1} - x_{3}$, where $x_{1}, x_{2}, x_{3}, x_{4}$ are the components of $x$.\n\n$T: \\begin{bmatrix}x_{1} \\\\ x_{2} \\\\ x_{3} \\\\ x_{4}\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x_{1} + x_{2} - x_{4} \\\\ 2x_{2} - x_{1} + 3x_{3} + 4x_{4} \\\\ 2x_{1} + x_{2} - x_{3} - 3x_{4} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}, b = \\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 6 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$"] [17592186125033 :question/answer-fields 17592186123220] [17592186125066 :question/problem "Given the transformation $T: \\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x + 2y \\\\ y - x + 6z \\\\ 8z - x + 3y\\end{bmatrix}$, find its rank."] [17592186124274 :question/problem "Let $\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}$ be a linear transformation. Determine the value of $k$ for which the vector $\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ k \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix}$ belongs to $\\textrm{Im}(\\mathbf{T})$."] [17592186124274 :question/worked-solution "The vector $\\mathbf{v}$ belongs to $\\textrm{Im}(\\mathbf{T})$ if there exists a vector $\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}$ such that\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{u}) = \\mathbf{v}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{bmatrix}x + y \\\\ -x + y \\\\ 2y\\end{bmatrix} &= \\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ k \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{x + y = 4; - x + y = k; 2y = 8\n$$\n\nFrom the third equation, we get $y=4$, which we substitute into the first equation to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx + y &= 4 \\\\\nx + 4 &= 4 \\\\\nx &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $x=0$ and $y=4$ into the third equation and obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-x + y &= k \\\\\n0 + 4 &= k \\\\\nk &= 4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\mathbf{v}\\in\\textrm{Im}(\\mathbf{T})$ when $k=4$."] [17592186124246 :question/problem "The standard matrix $T$ of a linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ and the vector $\\mathbf{b} \\in \\mathbb{R}^3$ are given below. Determine if there exists a vector $\\mathbf{x} \\in \\mathbb{R}^3$ such that $\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{x})=\\mathbf{b}$. In the affirmative case, find such a vector.\n\n$\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 6 & 4 & -10 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 8\\end{bmatrix}$"] [17592186124246 :question/worked-solution "Since $T$ is the standard matix of $\\mathbf{T}$, we have\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Im}(\\mathbf{T}) = \\textrm{Col}(T)\n$$\n\nIn order to determine whether $\\mathbf{b}$ lies in $\\textrm{Im}(\\mathbf{T})$, we need to check if the equation\n\n$$\nT\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}\n$$\n\nis consistent.\n\nSo, we find the row echelon form of the augmented matrix $\\begin{bmatrix}T \\, & \\, \\mathbf{b}\\end{bmatrix}$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{bmatrix}T & b\\end{bmatrix} &= \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 4 & 2 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 4 & -10 & 26 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 8 & -4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{vmatrix}R_{2}:=R_{2} + (-3)R_{1} \\\\ ∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 4 & 2 & 6 \\\\ 0 & -8 & -16 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 8 & -4\\end{bmatrix}\\end{vmatrix}R_{3}:=R_{3} + \\frac{1}{2}R_{2} \\\\\n&= ∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 4 & 2 & 6 \\\\ 0 & -8 & -16 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has a zero row, and so the system is consistent. We have two pivot columns (the $1$st and the $2$nd). So, $x_3$ is a free variable.\nNow, from the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n-8x_2-16x_3 = 8 \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad x_2=-1-2x_3\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation gives\n\n$$\n2x_1+4(-1-2x_3)+2x_3 = 6 \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad x_1=5+3x_3\n$$\n\nAs a result, the general solution is given by\n$\\begin{bmatrix}5 + 3x_{3} \\\\ -1 - 2x_{3} \\\\ x_{3}\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nSetting $x_3=0$, we have\n$\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$"] [17592186124247 :question/problem "If $\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 & -1 & -2 \\\\ -6 & 5 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 1 & 5 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$ is the standard matrix of the linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}$, then what is $\\text{rank}(\\mathbf{T})$?"] [17592186124247 :question/worked-solution "In general,\n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(\\mathbf{T}) = \\text{dim}(\\text{Im}(\\mathbf{T})) = \\text{rank}(T)\n$$\n\nSo, we need to find the rank of the matrix $T$. We reduce $T$ to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nT &= \\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 & -1 & -2 \\\\ -6 & 5 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 1 & 5 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{vmatrix}R_{2}:=R_{2} + 3R_{1} \\\\ R_{3}:=R_{3} + (-2)R_{1} \\\\ ∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 & -1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -4 & -4 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 7 & 7 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{vmatrix}R_{3}:=R_{3} + \\frac{7}{4}R_{2} \\\\\n&= ∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 & -1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -4 & -4 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, since $T$ has $2$ pivot columns, we have that\n\n$$\n\\textrm{rank}(\\mathbf{T})=\\textrm{rank}(T)=2\n$$"]] "immutable_answers" [17592186123209 17592186123210 17592186123211 17592186123212 17592186123213 17592186123221 17592186123222 17592186123223 17592186123224 17592186123225 17592186123311 17592186123312 17592186123313 17592186123314 17592186123315 17592186123317 17592186123318 17592186123319 17592186123320 17592186123321 17592186123419 17592186123420 17592186123421 17592186123422 17592186123423] "immutable_fields" [[17592186123208 :answer-field/correct] [17592186123208 :answer-field/id] [17592186123208 :answer-field/key] [17592186123208 :answer-field/type] [17592186123220 :answer-field/correct] [17592186123220 :answer-field/id] [17592186123220 :answer-field/key] [17592186123220 :answer-field/type] [17592186123310 :answer-field/correct] [17592186123310 :answer-field/id] [17592186123310 :answer-field/key] [17592186123310 :answer-field/type] [17592186123316 :answer-field/correct] [17592186123316 :answer-field/id] [17592186123316 :answer-field/key] [17592186123316 :answer-field/type] [17592186123418 :answer-field/correct] [17592186123418 :answer-field/id] [17592186123418 :answer-field/key] [17592186123418 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
